WEBVTT

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:08.174
["Saturdays" by Twin Shadow playing]

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:10.093
♪ This could be the last time ♪

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:13.680
♪ That could be
The fault line slipping away ♪

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:15.390
♪ This might be the last time ♪

00:00:15.473 --> 00:00:17.934
[woman] I've known Leonard since 2003.

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:20.311
It was wonderful
watching Leonard and Susan together.

00:00:20.395 --> 00:00:22.647
He was such an affectionate husband.

00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:23.940
♪ This will be a lost street ♪

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:25.734
♪ When you open your eyes ♪

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:27.444
[man] Leonard is a lot of fun.

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.529
He's a little quirky, but in a good way.

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:32.741
♪ Nothing's real ♪

00:00:32.824 --> 00:00:34.909
Susan will always be a part of his life.

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:36.369
Le Barricou on Grand.

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:38.788
♪ When you open your eyes, it's like ♪

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:44.252
♪ Saturdays, when we dance... ♪

00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:47.756
[woman] He's the kind of person
who is happiest in a relationship,

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:51.968
and so I hope he can find
someone worthy of his awesomeness.

00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:54.220
♪ Saturdays ♪

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:56.973
♪ When the moonlight lit up your face ♪

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:58.391
♪ And it all gets... ♪

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:00.435
[man] Leonard needs somebody special.

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:03.313
I hope Leonard has the happiness
he used to have.

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:05.356
♪ Saturdays ♪

00:01:06.274 --> 00:01:08.359
♪ When we dance in the dark in the room ♪

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:11.738
♪ Where it all gets real ♪

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.657
♪ I know you know how it feels ♪

00:01:15.366 --> 00:01:16.659
♪Saturdays ♪

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.621
♪ When the moonlight lit up your face ♪

00:01:19.704 --> 00:01:20.914
♪ And let love spark ♪

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:21.998
Hello.

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:24.501
-Hi. You must be Lauren?
-I'm Lauren.

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:27.754
-Leonard. Nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you, Leonard.

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:29.464
-Cool.
-Welcome.

00:01:29.547 --> 00:01:32.008
We're gonna do some fine dining tonight.
Is that correct?

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.218
Yes, ma'am.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:34.427
Okay.

00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:37.597
-Here?
-Looks like it.

00:01:42.352 --> 00:01:44.521
-It got late fast, huh?
-It did.

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:46.564
Have you ever been on a blind date before?

00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:48.691
-Not without knowing anything, I think.
-Right.

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.193
-Have you?
-Never.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:51.820
-Never?
-This is-- this is a first.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.823
-So, where do you live?
-Nolita. Do you know--

00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:55.990
Nolita? Sure.

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:58.034
-And where do you live?
-Upper West Side.

00:01:58.493 --> 00:02:00.954
Oh, I was terrified you were gonna be
from the Upper West Side.

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:02.330
Oh, yeah. Well, I...

00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:03.331
[laughing]

00:02:03.414 --> 00:02:05.416
And the only reason I was terrified,

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.254
I've met people from the Upper West Side.
I never seem to interest them.

00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:11.589
I have that problem
on the Upper East Side.

00:02:11.673 --> 00:02:13.424
[laughs] That's okay.

00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:15.135
It's like, hey, really.

00:02:15.218 --> 00:02:17.512
Nolita? No, that's, like, happening.

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:20.098
-Yeah.
-So, can we get some cocktails here?

00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:21.474
Yeah. What are you drinking?

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:28.481
-Do you have a San Siro, by any chance?
-We do.

00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:30.400
I would like a glass of the white wine.

00:02:30.775 --> 00:02:32.235
I think I'm gonna have a martini.

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:33.695
-What about you?
-The same.

00:02:33.778 --> 00:02:35.029
All right!

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.991
A vodka martini, straight up.

00:02:38.074 --> 00:02:39.993
-Straight-up?
-Extra olives, please.

00:02:40.076 --> 00:02:40.910
Okay.

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:44.747
Tito's martini straight up,
very dry with a twist.

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.499
-Be right back with that.
-Thanks.

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.419
Look at that.
Oh, it's so nice to meet you, Leonard.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.254
Yeah.

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.923
So, you were really late tonight,
you know?

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.885
Excuse me, I am never late.

00:02:56.968 --> 00:02:58.928
This is late for me
to have dinner, you know?

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.432
I eat like 6:00, 6:30.
I'm kind of boring sometimes,

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:05.435
'cause I like to take my teeth out
by eight o'clock.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:07.478
You know how it is.

00:03:07.562 --> 00:03:09.731
Yeah. So, you've always stayed
in New York?

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:11.566
No. I'm from Buffalo, New York.

00:03:11.941 --> 00:03:14.068
Buffalo, New York. I've been to Buffalo.

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:15.737
Why?

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.031
-[laughs]
-No. I live in Tribeca.

00:03:18.114 --> 00:03:21.075
I came here when I was 18 with $250

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:24.078
to become a famous artist, and it worked.

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:25.663
-It did?
-[laughs] Yeah.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.874
-Congratulations.
-Thank you very much.

00:03:28.291 --> 00:03:29.876
My late wife was an artist.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:31.920
Ah. You're a widower.

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:35.048
-So, I was married. I'm a widower.
-Oh, you are?

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:37.383
-That's right. You don't know anything--
-For how long?

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:38.718
Thirty-four years.

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:40.845
-When did your wife pass away?
-Three years ago.

00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:44.265
-Oh, I'm sorry.
-Yeah. I mean, I, um...

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.143
[sighs]

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:51.648
My husband recently passed away,
in October of 2016.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:53.650
-Sorry.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.068
And how did you guys meet?

00:03:55.151 --> 00:03:57.820
A blind date, sort of.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.531
-So you did have a blind date before.
-Well, it wasn't blind.

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:04.118
No, no, no, I owned
a self-portrait of hers before I met her,

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:07.163
and I had kind of fallen in love
with the self-portrait.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.957
-Aw, that's really sweet.
-Yeah.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.126
It's one of these, you know,
storybook things that worked out.

00:04:12.377 --> 00:04:15.505
-So you had a good marriage?
-Oh, yeah. I had a great marriage.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:17.465
Yeah, I was a lucky guy.

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:19.467
I can't even imagine.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.511
I sort of forgot I was getting divorced,

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.596
because it took eight years
to get divorced.

00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:27.267
I'd say anything to do
with the legal system is...

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:28.726
touch and go.

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:31.479
I was a lawyer, too, once in my life,

00:04:31.562 --> 00:04:33.147
-so look at all this--
-I'm so sorry.

00:04:33.231 --> 00:04:34.065
-Yeah.
-[laughs]

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:39.070
I'm embarrassed to tell you
I've been married three times.

00:04:39.153 --> 00:04:42.532
-Yeah.
-Yeah. So any of them any good?

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:45.076
They were all good.
I wouldn't have married them.

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:47.954
Yeah. No, I liked... I loved them.
I like them,

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:50.540
and then I love them,
and then I like them.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:51.624
They still around?

00:04:52.375 --> 00:04:54.294
They're still alive. I didn't kill any.

00:04:54.377 --> 00:04:56.170
-No--
-They didn't piss me off that much.

00:04:56.254 --> 00:04:57.088
[laughs]

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.590
-You would have liked me as a lawyer.
-You were a lawyer?

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:01.426
-You really were?
-I was a good-guy lawyer.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:03.428
-What kind of law?
-Public-interest litigation.

00:05:03.511 --> 00:05:06.389
So I was the president
of the board of Greenpeace.

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:09.350
-A true believer.
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:12.228
-And you? Yeah? Great.
-Hell yeah. Of course.

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:13.396
You know, for that...

00:05:14.063 --> 00:05:16.441
little magical time,
I was involved in it all,

00:05:16.524 --> 00:05:18.443
and then I walked away from it.

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.696
-Thanks.
-There you go. And for you, sir.

00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:22.822
Thanks so much.

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:24.824
-Cheers.
-Yeah.

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:27.201
-This is good. Cheers.
-Yeah.

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:29.037
-To the date?
-Absolutely.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.372
-So nice to meet you.
-I've been looking forward to it.

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:33.791
-I'm having a good time already.
-Yeah, me too.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.419
It seems that way. Good.

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:39.547
I wasn't sure that I...
that that would happen. That's nice.

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:44.052
I would think that somebody
who, like, divorced three men

00:05:44.135 --> 00:05:45.720
is probably pretty picky.

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:49.349
Um... Well, I married them.
They all asked me, and I said yes,

00:05:49.432 --> 00:05:51.559
so I guess I wasn't
that picky at the time.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:53.394
I just say yes to everything.

00:05:53.478 --> 00:05:54.645
[both laugh]

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:56.731
-Don't ask me, 'cause I'm not looking.
-Not tonight.

00:05:56.814 --> 00:05:59.817
-I won't ask you tonight.
-I'm not looking for another husband.

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:01.069
I want a partner,

00:06:01.152 --> 00:06:03.988
someone that I can travel
and, you know, do fun things with.

00:06:04.072 --> 00:06:05.031
Yeah, me too.

00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:06.824
I haven't been single very often.

00:06:07.492 --> 00:06:11.746
-Aha!
-I tend to be a one-man woman.

00:06:11.829 --> 00:06:14.165
-Right.
-All I ever wanted to do was be married

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:17.001
and have children.
That was my main goal in life.

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:18.878
-And do you have children?
-I have one girl.

00:06:18.961 --> 00:06:20.338
Uh-huh. And where is she?

00:06:20.797 --> 00:06:22.673
She's currently living at home with me,

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:24.592
but she's more like my roommate.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.385
Wait a second, this isn't going right.

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:28.721
You know much more about me
than I know about you.

00:06:28.805 --> 00:06:30.223
Well, that's what I do for a living.

00:06:30.681 --> 00:06:31.641
You like to listen?

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:34.310
I'm a private investigator.
I ask a lot of questions.

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:36.521
-Oh.
-I'm a private investigator.

00:06:36.604 --> 00:06:37.438
[laughs]

00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:39.690
I swear it's true.

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:42.151
Why would you believe that, you know?

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:44.404
That's always my dream, you know, to be...

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:46.155
-Everybody says that.
-...a PI.

00:06:46.823 --> 00:06:49.534
-So what do you do?
-I sell real estate.

00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:52.537
-I taught health and phys ed.
-I was in the fashion business.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.539
-Does not surprise me.
-You can...

00:06:54.622 --> 00:06:55.790
Hello. Looking good.

00:06:55.873 --> 00:06:56.749
Yeah.

00:06:56.833 --> 00:06:59.836
Gym teachers is what we used to call them
when I was in school, right?

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:02.630
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. I was a gym teacher.
-You were a gym teacher.

00:07:02.713 --> 00:07:03.589
[both laugh]

00:07:03.673 --> 00:07:05.800
Are you into fashion or--

00:07:06.217 --> 00:07:10.138
-Ah... this is fashion to me.
-Is that as fashion as you get?

00:07:10.513 --> 00:07:11.722
'Cause I like a guy--

00:07:12.014 --> 00:07:15.184
I like-- I think it's really sexy
when a man wears a suit,

00:07:15.476 --> 00:07:17.812
like a black suit
with a white shirt and a tie.

00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:20.356
Uh, yeah. I'd have to probably go buy one,

00:07:20.815 --> 00:07:23.776
so it would have to be, like,
a really special second date.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.945
-Hello, I'm worth it.
-That's right. Yeah, yeah.

00:07:26.362 --> 00:07:27.488
[laughs]

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:32.034
-Hi.
-We do have a table ready

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:33.744
for you inside as soon as you're ready.

00:07:33.828 --> 00:07:36.581
-Thank you.
-Good, because the wind started to blow.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.300
-Hi, I'm Dianna.
-Hi. I'm Leonard.

00:07:47.383 --> 00:07:49.635
-Hi, Leonard. Nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you.

00:07:52.054 --> 00:07:54.682
Could there be a stranger way
of meeting someone?

00:07:56.017 --> 00:07:57.310
No, this is it.

00:07:57.393 --> 00:07:59.812
-Right?
-Top of the list.

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.983
-So, welcome.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:08:04.358 --> 00:08:05.526
So, Leonard.

00:08:06.235 --> 00:08:07.069
I-I--

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:09.197
My middle son is Leonard.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.282
-How many you got?
-I have three children.

00:08:11.365 --> 00:08:12.200
-Three sons?
-No.

00:08:12.283 --> 00:08:14.368
Two sons and a daughter.

00:08:14.744 --> 00:08:15.578
Wow.

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.330
-Yeah. How about you?
-None.

00:08:17.622 --> 00:08:19.957
-None? Okay.
-Zero, yeah.

00:08:20.041 --> 00:08:23.753
We celebrated my birthday yesterday
with my sons,

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:25.630
-and then I'm going to--
-Happy birthday.

00:08:25.713 --> 00:08:28.466
-Thank you. It's actually tomorrow.
-Happy birthday.

00:08:28.549 --> 00:08:31.010
-Thank you.
-Well, it's a nice celebration.

00:08:31.093 --> 00:08:32.011
Thank you.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:33.554
[laughs]

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:39.727
-Is this a favorite place of yours then?
-I like this place.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.812
I brought the wrong glasses,
so I can't see anything.

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:43.189
I didn't bring any glasses,

00:08:43.272 --> 00:08:45.316
-but I can read it.
-Neither of us can see anything?

00:08:45.816 --> 00:08:47.151
Neither one of us can read.

00:08:47.235 --> 00:08:48.861
-Okay. I can help you.
-I can--

00:08:48.945 --> 00:08:50.780
-Oh, you can?
-Yeah. I can actually read that.

00:08:51.364 --> 00:08:53.449
-There's... escargot.
-[laughing]

00:08:54.325 --> 00:08:55.993
-Really?
-Oysters...

00:08:56.285 --> 00:08:57.620
and a salad

00:08:57.703 --> 00:09:01.707
that I can't read the ingredients, because
they're smaller than the word "salad."

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:05.044
-I love oysters.
-Let's start there.

00:09:05.336 --> 00:09:06.170
Cool.

00:09:07.296 --> 00:09:08.256
Okay.

00:09:08.464 --> 00:09:10.967
So, what else?
Tell me something exciting about yourself.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:14.345
No, no, I want to hear, like,
about these three husbands of yours.

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:17.598
So tell me about, like,
husband number one.

00:09:17.682 --> 00:09:20.184
-When was that?
-Husband number one was a good guy,

00:09:20.268 --> 00:09:23.229
very sweet,
but we just kind of grew apart.

00:09:23.312 --> 00:09:24.480
Second husband,

00:09:24.564 --> 00:09:26.023
he was, like, woo,

00:09:26.107 --> 00:09:28.150
wacky, so that didn't last long.

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:31.195
And then the third husband,
I just couldn't live with him.

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:35.950
He actually had in his last house a sofa
sitting on the lawn.

00:09:36.033 --> 00:09:37.118
I was like, "No."

00:09:37.827 --> 00:09:39.120
-That wouldn't be for you.
-No.

00:09:39.203 --> 00:09:42.832
-'Cause I got class with a "K," baby.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.167
So, I'm a widower.

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:46.711
-Okay.
-You?

00:09:47.378 --> 00:09:51.465
Divorced. And I'm sorry
to hear that you're a widow.

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:54.427
-Yeah.
-What did she die of?

00:09:55.136 --> 00:09:56.012
Cancer...

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:57.388
ovarian.

00:09:57.471 --> 00:09:59.181
I'm so sorry for your loss.

00:09:59.432 --> 00:10:01.058
Yeah. She was a good woman.

00:10:02.476 --> 00:10:03.519
I really liked her.

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:07.481
But there's grief in divorce, too.

00:10:08.357 --> 00:10:10.985
-Different. Different kind of grief.
-Different, but yeah.

00:10:11.068 --> 00:10:12.737
See, my husband had a stroke,

00:10:13.321 --> 00:10:15.781
and for about--
a little less than three years,

00:10:16.198 --> 00:10:18.492
I was very devoted to all the caregiving.

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:20.244
[exhales]

00:10:20.328 --> 00:10:21.495
He died, though, right?

00:10:22.038 --> 00:10:22.955
He's not alive.

00:10:23.039 --> 00:10:24.749
-I'm not cheating on him at the moment.
-No.

00:10:24.832 --> 00:10:27.335
Just checking, the way you're talking.

00:10:27.418 --> 00:10:31.672
Well, I'm preparing you for the fact
that while he was still alive,

00:10:31.881 --> 00:10:34.175
I did start to date...

00:10:34.258 --> 00:10:35.885
-Uh-huh.
-...a particular person,

00:10:35.968 --> 00:10:37.803
and my husband was...

00:10:38.846 --> 00:10:39.805
jealous,

00:10:39.889 --> 00:10:42.183
which was a good position to put him in.

00:10:42.266 --> 00:10:43.476
He needed some of that.

00:10:43.559 --> 00:10:46.020
Wait, wait. He had a stroke.
What did he need that for?

00:10:46.103 --> 00:10:47.521
He finally appreciated me.

00:10:47.813 --> 00:10:52.151
And I wish I could have known
that giving him a little sense of jealousy

00:10:52.401 --> 00:10:55.446
-would have--
-Well, he's a guy who's had a stroke.

00:10:55.529 --> 00:10:58.741
Like, he wasn't in, like,
a bad enough place.

00:11:00.701 --> 00:11:03.162
So, do you have a big apartment?
Is it... How big?

00:11:03.245 --> 00:11:06.957
-Nineteen-hundred square feet.
-Whoa!

00:11:07.041 --> 00:11:08.209
Oh, my goodness!

00:11:08.292 --> 00:11:09.877
You're never moving from there.

00:11:10.419 --> 00:11:11.837
I don't know. It's on the market.

00:11:12.213 --> 00:11:14.090
-Oh, you own it. You're not renting?
-Yeah.

00:11:14.173 --> 00:11:15.174
Oh!

00:11:15.257 --> 00:11:18.052
So you like me more
even though I don't have a suit on?

00:11:18.469 --> 00:11:19.720
Is that it?

00:11:19.804 --> 00:11:23.307
-Well, you know, that adds to the allure.
-Allure. Yeah.

00:11:23.391 --> 00:11:25.518
Square footage in New York City

00:11:25.601 --> 00:11:27.937
is definitely valuable.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.732
-Everything's very expensive.
-It's ridiculous in the city right now.

00:11:31.816 --> 00:11:35.111
Well, there's really beautiful buildings,
like, with elevators.

00:11:35.611 --> 00:11:36.529
[laughs]

00:11:36.612 --> 00:11:40.574
For 43 years, I've walked 60 steps up
and 60 steps down.

00:11:40.658 --> 00:11:42.368
Yeah. I've got an elevator, so I'm--

00:11:42.702 --> 00:11:43.869
Ah, you pussy.

00:11:44.078 --> 00:11:45.371
[laughs]

00:11:52.795 --> 00:11:53.963
[woman] Let me move these.

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:55.881
Sorry, it's a little large.

00:11:56.924 --> 00:11:58.634
[Karen] Great. Awesome.

00:11:58.843 --> 00:12:00.052
Thank you.

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:04.223
-[Leonard] Enjoy.
-Thank you. Same to you.

00:12:04.515 --> 00:12:07.601
-I can't get my oyster out.
-Yeah. I'm gonna just have to--

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:09.937
Yeah, that's all right. Mmm.

00:12:11.021 --> 00:12:13.733
So, I used to be a cocktail waitress
at the Oyster Bar.

00:12:14.275 --> 00:12:15.234
[laughs]

00:12:15.317 --> 00:12:16.652
-In Grand Central?
-Yeah.

00:12:16.736 --> 00:12:18.738
-Really?
-Yeah. It was great.

00:12:19.405 --> 00:12:22.283
-Right after I graduated from college.
-I like that place.

00:12:23.742 --> 00:12:25.411
I grew up in a very...

00:12:26.203 --> 00:12:27.163
sheltered...

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:28.539
uh, life.

00:12:28.622 --> 00:12:29.748
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:12:30.166 --> 00:12:33.794
My mother and my, um, stepfather

00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:35.254
were both deaf.

00:12:35.337 --> 00:12:36.756
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:38.757
So my first language is sign.

00:12:39.049 --> 00:12:41.635
-Wow.
-And not until I left home,

00:12:42.136 --> 00:12:44.346
that's when it was like the whole world...

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:45.973
opened up for me.

00:12:46.056 --> 00:12:47.433
It was like I was born.

00:12:47.808 --> 00:12:49.602
And it was the '70s, right?

00:12:49.685 --> 00:12:50.811
Drugs and rock 'n' roll.

00:12:50.895 --> 00:12:51.854
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:53.397
-It was great.
-It was great, right?

00:12:53.481 --> 00:12:54.356
I loved it.

00:12:55.608 --> 00:12:58.444
-I've never done a drug. And you?
-Oh, yeah.

00:12:58.527 --> 00:12:59.403
I loved them.

00:12:59.487 --> 00:13:00.404
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:13:00.738 --> 00:13:01.572
They were great.

00:13:01.864 --> 00:13:04.158
And what was the first one you tried?

00:13:04.241 --> 00:13:05.075
LSD.

00:13:05.951 --> 00:13:08.078
-[Gloria] You've taken LSD?
-Mmm.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:09.246
Yes?

00:13:09.538 --> 00:13:10.456
Oh, my God.

00:13:10.539 --> 00:13:13.501
This conversation just took
a really interesting turn.

00:13:13.584 --> 00:13:15.044
[laughs] I like this.

00:13:15.127 --> 00:13:17.796
I'm sorry, but this is very new to me.
Like, I don't--

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:19.423
None of my friends do drugs.

00:13:19.507 --> 00:13:21.509
-Oh, really?
-I don't do drugs. No.

00:13:21.592 --> 00:13:23.886
I'm, like, "the goody girl." [laughs]

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:26.222
Would you do it again?
You have any with you?

00:13:26.305 --> 00:13:27.139
[both laughing]

00:13:27.223 --> 00:13:28.265
Oh, this is so interesting.

00:13:28.349 --> 00:13:29.183
[laughs]

00:13:29.767 --> 00:13:32.394
-I think they made me a nicer person.
-[Karen] Really?

00:13:32.478 --> 00:13:34.355
I think I'm pretty nice
and I didn't do drugs.

00:13:34.438 --> 00:13:37.566
I can't imagine what I'd be like
if I did them. [laughs]

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:39.902
-Drugs and rock 'n' roll.
-Drugs and rock 'n' roll.

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:40.819
Yeah.

00:13:41.445 --> 00:13:42.488
[laughs]

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:45.741
So what do you want to get
out of the dating scene?

00:13:45.824 --> 00:13:47.910
I want to, like, fall in love. I want to--

00:13:47.993 --> 00:13:49.203
-You do?
-Oh, yeah.

00:13:49.286 --> 00:13:51.413
-[message alert]
-You want to fall in love. My phone!

00:13:51.872 --> 00:13:53.541
-So--
-I wonder who that is.

00:13:55.251 --> 00:13:57.711
Oh, my daughter wants to know
how it's going.

00:13:58.087 --> 00:13:59.255
[laughs]

00:13:59.338 --> 00:14:00.881
-Tell her it's going great.
-Going great?

00:14:00.965 --> 00:14:02.424
Oh, this is so funny.

00:14:03.551 --> 00:14:05.302
You don't have a phone. What, you don't--

00:14:05.386 --> 00:14:08.305
I do have a phone, but, you know,
I don't take it out on dates.

00:14:08.389 --> 00:14:10.683
-It's kind of rude. I agree.
-Kind of rude, yeah.

00:14:10.766 --> 00:14:12.685
I didn't want to say rude, but you did.

00:14:12.768 --> 00:14:14.311
-That's okay.
-I'm just, you know--

00:14:14.395 --> 00:14:15.938
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:18.816
-[message alert]
-Oh, oh, let's see what Maria has to say.

00:14:19.608 --> 00:14:20.651
Oh! [laughs]

00:14:21.277 --> 00:14:23.112
"Wow. Say hi to him for me."

00:14:23.195 --> 00:14:24.905
[laughs] That's very sweet, Maria.

00:14:26.073 --> 00:14:27.199
Oh, okay.

00:14:29.285 --> 00:14:30.578
What's that tattoo?

00:14:30.661 --> 00:14:32.663
This says, "Carpe diem,"

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:35.708
which means "seize the day."
I think you know what it means.

00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:37.376
That's my bat.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:39.920
What do you mean by "your bat"?

00:14:40.254 --> 00:14:42.882
It's my bat. Bat. I like bats.

00:14:43.507 --> 00:14:44.675
[message alert]

00:14:44.758 --> 00:14:46.302
-[Leonard] Um...
-My daughter.

00:14:48.178 --> 00:14:49.138
Well...

00:14:50.139 --> 00:14:51.765
[Gloria laughing]

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.228
-You have any tattoos?
-No. Those are nice.

00:14:56.312 --> 00:14:58.689
You should get one.
How come you don't have any?

00:14:58.772 --> 00:15:03.485
'Cause I haven't, like, come up with
what I would, like, put on myself forever.

00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:07.072
You're already, like, in your 60s.
It's not like you got that much more time.

00:15:08.908 --> 00:15:11.952
I can't believe
she is texting me on my date,

00:15:12.036 --> 00:15:14.705
because I told her I wasn't even
gonna have my phone out.

00:15:14.788 --> 00:15:17.374
-[message alert]
-Is this disturbing you? A little bit?

00:15:17.666 --> 00:15:20.002
-[Leonard] Um...
-I'm worried now.

00:15:20.085 --> 00:15:21.253
I wouldn't do it, but--

00:15:21.545 --> 00:15:23.297
Oh, shit. [laughs]

00:15:24.048 --> 00:15:25.966
So, like, what are you looking for?

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:29.887
Oh, so, I look at politics,
and I look at religion.

00:15:30.262 --> 00:15:32.306
-Like, liberal minimum.
-Mm-hmm.

00:15:32.389 --> 00:15:34.767
And, you know, I'm an atheist, so--

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.852
Which I am not. [laughs]

00:15:36.935 --> 00:15:39.521
-So, what are you?
-Very, very spiritual.

00:15:39.605 --> 00:15:42.524
-That's huge for me.
-See, I think I'm spiritual, too.

00:15:42.608 --> 00:15:47.446
But I don't think you need an entity
to feel like we're all connected.

00:15:47.738 --> 00:15:49.073
So, let me stop you there.

00:15:49.156 --> 00:15:50.324
We have a lot of differences.

00:15:50.407 --> 00:15:52.785
-We know we've got a lot of differences.
-Yeah, right.

00:15:52.868 --> 00:15:54.078
[laughs]

00:15:54.536 --> 00:15:55.829
I was a bar mitzvah boy.

00:15:55.913 --> 00:15:58.207
-I went to an Orthodox Hebrew school.
-Okay.

00:15:58.666 --> 00:15:59.875
But they made me go.

00:16:00.834 --> 00:16:01.710
My, um...

00:16:01.961 --> 00:16:03.837
children were raised Jewish,

00:16:04.588 --> 00:16:07.383
so they had bar mitzvahs
and a bat mitzvah.

00:16:08.759 --> 00:16:11.804
Well, sorry you're out
with another Jew. [laughs]

00:16:12.763 --> 00:16:14.264
You haven't had much luck with them.

00:16:16.809 --> 00:16:19.853
-Maybe we can think of, like, who you--
-We can help each other.

00:16:19.937 --> 00:16:21.814
-We can help each other of with, like...
-Yeah.

00:16:21.897 --> 00:16:24.650
-who would be a great fit for each other?
-Yeah.

00:16:25.025 --> 00:16:29.238
-I actually have, like, a list that--
-I know one that a friend just divorced.

00:16:29.488 --> 00:16:30.656
-There we go.
-Yeah.

00:16:30.739 --> 00:16:33.659
-[laughs]
-He's a contractor. He can build.

00:16:33.742 --> 00:16:36.745
My wife thought he was, like,
one of the sexiest guys, too.

00:16:37.705 --> 00:16:39.540
So, do you have a picture of your friend?

00:16:39.623 --> 00:16:41.750
[both laughing]

00:16:42.001 --> 00:16:44.128
So, have you heard any good jokes lately?

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.422
I am not, you know...

00:16:46.839 --> 00:16:47.715
Um, wait.

00:16:48.132 --> 00:16:50.008
Let's see if I can remember it.

00:16:50.551 --> 00:16:51.802
So, this... [laughs]

00:16:52.219 --> 00:16:55.556
uh, professor, this Swedish professor,
is, um...

00:16:55.889 --> 00:16:57.182
teaching a, um...

00:16:57.725 --> 00:16:58.934
biology class.

00:16:59.017 --> 00:17:02.730
So, he's got a frog,
and he cuts off a leg of the frog,

00:17:02.813 --> 00:17:05.691
and he says, "Yump, froggy, yump,"

00:17:05.774 --> 00:17:07.401
and so the frog jumps.

00:17:07.693 --> 00:17:09.987
And he's got three legs.
Just jumps. No problem.

00:17:10.070 --> 00:17:12.781
So, the professor cuts off
another leg and he says,

00:17:13.031 --> 00:17:14.408
"Yump, froggy, yump."

00:17:14.491 --> 00:17:16.243
And the frog jumps, you know,

00:17:16.326 --> 00:17:17.578
with a little, "Uh!"

00:17:17.661 --> 00:17:19.371
-Just a little harder.
-Oh, gosh.

00:17:19.455 --> 00:17:21.832
Then he cuts off a third leg and he says,

00:17:21.915 --> 00:17:23.333
"Yump, froggy, yump."

00:17:23.417 --> 00:17:24.626
And then... [groaning]

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.044
He just struggles.

00:17:26.128 --> 00:17:28.589
But the frog jumps a little bit more.

00:17:29.256 --> 00:17:32.051
And then he cuts off
the last leg and he goes,

00:17:32.301 --> 00:17:33.594
"Yump, froggy, yump."

00:17:33.677 --> 00:17:34.511
And...

00:17:34.845 --> 00:17:36.555
the frog can't jump, and he says,

00:17:36.722 --> 00:17:37.639
"See?

00:17:37.723 --> 00:17:39.892
Frog with no legs is deaf."

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:47.608
Want to get some dessert? Finish up here
and go have some dessert somewhere else?

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:50.861
Well, not now, 'cause I want to go to bed.
I want to sleep.

00:17:51.195 --> 00:17:52.738
Do you have an after-dinner drink?

00:17:52.821 --> 00:17:54.198
No.

00:17:55.449 --> 00:17:56.742
Can I get you a cab?

00:17:56.825 --> 00:17:57.951
That would be great.

00:17:58.410 --> 00:17:59.244
Okay.

00:17:59.828 --> 00:18:01.538
So let's, um... go for a walk.

00:18:01.622 --> 00:18:02.664
-Okay.
-Great.

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:05.834
-Can I help you with your shawl?
-Yes.

00:18:07.836 --> 00:18:10.422
-I don't think I've ever done this before.
-[laughs]

00:18:10.714 --> 00:18:12.466
-Thank you.
-My pleasure.

00:18:12.549 --> 00:18:14.635
-Do you say that to all the girls?
-Yeah.

00:18:14.718 --> 00:18:16.762
-I'm just a bad boy.
-Huh.

00:18:17.638 --> 00:18:20.140
-Can I put you in a cab?
-You can.

00:18:20.432 --> 00:18:22.309
Wow. You walk fast.

00:18:30.651 --> 00:18:32.319
What is a good date to you?

00:18:32.402 --> 00:18:34.404
Like, to me, a good date
would be to go on a dance.

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:38.659
Really? So that would never, like--
that would never cross my mind,

00:18:39.076 --> 00:18:41.328
'cause disco's been over for a while.

00:18:41.411 --> 00:18:43.121
It would not be disco.

00:18:43.413 --> 00:18:45.082
I like partner dancing.

00:18:45.165 --> 00:18:48.001
Yeah, so I don't do much of that.

00:18:50.087 --> 00:18:51.088
It's, uh...

00:18:51.755 --> 00:18:54.341
It's a beautiful night for a birthday.
Is it--

00:18:54.424 --> 00:18:57.678
-It must be, like, almost your birthday.
-We're getting there.

00:18:57.761 --> 00:19:00.305
-Although I think... What time is it?
-Wait, wait...

00:19:00.389 --> 00:19:03.100
-It's... Happy birthday!
-[gasps] Thank you!

00:19:03.183 --> 00:19:04.017
Oh, my God.

00:19:04.101 --> 00:19:05.018
Thank you.

00:19:05.102 --> 00:19:06.270
-So, um--
-Thank you.

00:19:06.353 --> 00:19:07.479
Happy birth--

00:19:07.938 --> 00:19:09.773
♪ Happy birthday to-- ♪

00:19:09.857 --> 00:19:11.024
I really can't sing.

00:19:11.275 --> 00:19:13.026
So, how do you sign it?

00:19:14.111 --> 00:19:16.155
Happy... birthday.

00:19:16.238 --> 00:19:17.865
-This is "happy."
-Happy.

00:19:17.948 --> 00:19:20.409
And, then, "birthday" is...
I just spell it out.

00:19:20.993 --> 00:19:22.411
Yeah, which... Yeah.

00:19:22.619 --> 00:19:24.663
-Happy birthday to me.
-Happy birthday.

00:19:24.746 --> 00:19:25.581
Happy, happy.

00:19:25.664 --> 00:19:26.957
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:19:28.041 --> 00:19:31.003
So, will you memorize this joke
and not ever tell the frog joke again?

00:19:31.086 --> 00:19:32.629
-Go ahead. I'm ready.
-So...

00:19:33.005 --> 00:19:34.506
what do a walrus...

00:19:35.549 --> 00:19:38.760
and a Tupperware saleslady
have in common?

00:19:38.927 --> 00:19:41.179
A walrus and a Tupperware sales--

00:19:41.388 --> 00:19:42.264
A walrus?

00:19:43.265 --> 00:19:44.266
I don't know.

00:19:45.142 --> 00:19:47.477
They're both looking for a tight seal.

00:19:49.396 --> 00:19:50.564
[laughs]

00:19:50.647 --> 00:19:53.358
A walrus is looking for a tight seal.

00:19:53.775 --> 00:19:55.694
-I don't--
-And a Tupperware saleslady.

00:19:55.777 --> 00:19:56.820
That, I get that.

00:19:56.904 --> 00:19:59.072
-Yeah.
-But why is the walrus--

00:19:59.156 --> 00:20:00.616
Looking for a tight seal?

00:20:00.699 --> 00:20:02.701
Oh, a tight seal! Oh, great.

00:20:02.784 --> 00:20:03.744
Oh.

00:20:04.453 --> 00:20:05.454
Forgive me.

00:20:07.623 --> 00:20:08.874
[Gloria] Do you have rhythm?

00:20:09.166 --> 00:20:12.085
Well, could you-- Let me see something.
I'll be able to tell.

00:20:12.169 --> 00:20:15.589
Just make-believe there's slow music
and dance with me.

00:20:15.672 --> 00:20:17.090
-Yeah.
-And I want to see...

00:20:17.174 --> 00:20:18.425
Should I give you a hint?

00:20:19.092 --> 00:20:21.345
-I'll give you a hint.
-You don't have to give me a hint.

00:20:21.428 --> 00:20:23.263
-I can't?
-You can give me a hint, but--

00:20:23.347 --> 00:20:24.640
-I think I should.
-Yeah.

00:20:24.723 --> 00:20:26.934
I'm gonna say, "Right, left,
right, left, right." Ready?

00:20:27.017 --> 00:20:28.393
-Okay.
-Right, left.

00:20:28.477 --> 00:20:29.478
No, that's my left.

00:20:29.561 --> 00:20:32.105
-Oh, 'cause--
-So I figured to teach, you got to...

00:20:32.231 --> 00:20:33.815
-You got to do the opposition.
-Yeah.

00:20:33.899 --> 00:20:36.068
-Okay, ready? Left, right...
-Yeah.

00:20:36.318 --> 00:20:38.362
-left, right, left.
-Oh, we're back to Lindy.

00:20:38.445 --> 00:20:39.780
Left, right,

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:41.698
left, right, left.

00:20:42.032 --> 00:20:42.908
-Like that?
-Yeah.

00:20:42.991 --> 00:20:44.076
Oh, that's nice.

00:20:44.159 --> 00:20:45.619
-And then like that.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:20:45.702 --> 00:20:47.454
And... over your head.

00:20:47.537 --> 00:20:49.456
There you go. Let go.

00:20:49.706 --> 00:20:51.458
And now we'll go back to Lindy. [laughs]

00:20:51.541 --> 00:20:53.126
Oh, yeah, then that's it.

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:55.754
-Yeah, but it's nice.
-Yeah. No, I get it.

00:20:55.837 --> 00:20:58.215
I get it. I get it. I do get it.

00:21:00.425 --> 00:21:02.094
-Yeah.
-Thank you.

00:21:03.303 --> 00:21:06.807
-So what are you gonna have?
-I think I'm gonna have a scotch. And you?

00:21:07.057 --> 00:21:08.058
-Bourbon.
-Bourbon?

00:21:08.141 --> 00:21:10.227
What about making a wish?

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:11.228
[laughs]

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:12.354
All right.

00:21:12.437 --> 00:21:14.106
Oh, let's see.

00:21:14.523 --> 00:21:15.440
Um...

00:21:15.524 --> 00:21:16.358
So...

00:21:16.441 --> 00:21:18.026
-Good health.
-Uh-uh.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:19.444
-Love.
-Yeah.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.489
Financial abundance and happiness.

00:21:22.781 --> 00:21:24.741
-That's your wish?
-On my birthday, yes.

00:21:25.409 --> 00:21:26.785
Blow out the candle.

00:21:27.744 --> 00:21:29.746
Happy birthday. May they all come true,

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:31.832
or may they remain true.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:34.626
Can we drink to the...

00:21:35.377 --> 00:21:36.461
to the frog?

00:21:37.629 --> 00:21:39.631
And, oh,
you may never tell that joke again.

00:21:39.715 --> 00:21:41.967
You're supposed to tell
the walrus joke now.

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:45.012
If I don't tell the joke again,
can we drink to the frog,

00:21:45.262 --> 00:21:46.304
the poor frog?

00:21:46.888 --> 00:21:48.890
-No, okay.
-How about, um...

00:21:49.182 --> 00:21:50.100
meeting each other?

00:21:50.183 --> 00:21:51.768
-Oh, good idea.
-How about that?

00:21:52.102 --> 00:21:53.562
-To us.
-Our first date.

00:21:53.645 --> 00:21:54.563
Our first date.

00:21:54.646 --> 00:21:56.189
-Okay.
-[laughs]

00:21:56.565 --> 00:21:57.524
I love that.

00:21:58.358 --> 00:21:59.192
Mmm.

00:21:59.276 --> 00:22:01.028
[Gloria] I haven't dated
in a very long time.

00:22:01.737 --> 00:22:04.740
Do I seem like somebody who hasn't
been on a date for years and years?

00:22:04.823 --> 00:22:06.199
No, not at all.

00:22:06.283 --> 00:22:09.161
What I basically do is hope
somebody will ask me on a date.

00:22:09.244 --> 00:22:12.664
I don't like the idea
of being the pursuer.

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:14.374
Well...

00:22:15.542 --> 00:22:17.669
-get over it, you know?
-[laughs]

00:22:17.753 --> 00:22:21.089
-It's...
-Is that... it's a new time?

00:22:21.173 --> 00:22:25.302
It's a new time, and we're old, you know?

00:22:25.385 --> 00:22:27.346
How much time do you have?

00:22:28.013 --> 00:22:29.056
So...

00:22:29.431 --> 00:22:34.478
Have you gone out with a lot of men
who are not, um, African-American?

00:22:35.395 --> 00:22:38.482
I tend these days, uh, to...

00:22:38.899 --> 00:22:41.151
prefer to date black men.

00:22:41.234 --> 00:22:43.487
You know, I've been out with, like, one...

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:45.030
-African-American woman.
-Oh, yeah?

00:22:45.113 --> 00:22:47.199
-It was like 1969.
-Okay.

00:22:47.282 --> 00:22:49.242
And I was just, like, so in love,

00:22:49.326 --> 00:22:52.162
and, you know, it was like
Black Power movement time.

00:22:52.245 --> 00:22:55.248
She said, "Do you think
I can take you home to meet my parents?"

00:22:55.332 --> 00:22:57.626
You know, it was, like, not a possibility.

00:22:57.834 --> 00:23:02.255
Well, that would not have been
my experience with my... uh, parents.

00:23:02.339 --> 00:23:04.049
It's so sad, right?

00:23:07.636 --> 00:23:10.138
I'm gonna do a drawing for you
in lipstick.

00:23:10.847 --> 00:23:12.557
Is it L-E-N-N-Y?

00:23:13.433 --> 00:23:15.310
Uh, it's L-E-O-N-A-R-D.

00:23:16.812 --> 00:23:17.813
[woman] Last call.

00:23:18.688 --> 00:23:19.648
-Hey.
-Thank you.

00:23:20.649 --> 00:23:22.567
[laughs] Make it a double.

00:23:23.026 --> 00:23:24.194
[laughs] Yeah.

00:23:24.653 --> 00:23:26.822
-A woman after my own heart.
-[laughs]

00:23:29.449 --> 00:23:33.078
But anyway, I feel like these are our last
minutes together and we need to, like...

00:23:33.161 --> 00:23:34.121
Eat dessert.

00:23:34.579 --> 00:23:35.414
Sure.

00:23:35.956 --> 00:23:37.707
-But also, maybe just--
-We'll take--

00:23:37.791 --> 00:23:40.877
Is there anything that's not been said
that you wanted to say?

00:23:41.461 --> 00:23:44.047
I'm like... I don't know what to do
at the end of a date.

00:23:44.131 --> 00:23:45.048
Neither do I.

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:46.258
[laughing]

00:23:47.759 --> 00:23:48.593
Thank you.

00:23:49.136 --> 00:23:50.512
-My pleasure.
-It's been wonderful.

00:23:50.595 --> 00:23:51.972
Yeah, I had a great time.

00:23:52.681 --> 00:23:54.808
-That's "thank you" in sign language.
-Oh, yeah?

00:23:54.891 --> 00:23:57.519
Oh, that's a beautiful way
to say "thank you," right?

00:23:58.437 --> 00:23:59.354
Yeah.

00:24:00.063 --> 00:24:00.939
Here.

00:24:01.148 --> 00:24:02.357
That's beautiful.

00:24:02.732 --> 00:24:03.650
[giggles]

00:24:03.733 --> 00:24:04.568
Thank you.

00:24:06.653 --> 00:24:07.863
It's really something.

00:24:10.031 --> 00:24:11.533
It really is something.

00:24:13.702 --> 00:24:16.121
-So, you ready?
-[Dianna] Yes.

00:24:17.289 --> 00:24:18.373
You look beautiful.

00:24:18.456 --> 00:24:19.708
-Aw, thank you.
-Yeah.

00:24:32.971 --> 00:24:35.432
So, do you think I talked too much
on this date?

00:24:35.932 --> 00:24:39.895
Not at all. Why would you even, like,
think that as a possibility?

00:24:39.978 --> 00:24:41.229
That I talk too much?

00:24:41.605 --> 00:24:43.023
No. No.

00:24:43.106 --> 00:24:44.483
-I don't know.
-You're wrong, no.

00:24:44.566 --> 00:24:47.110
You haven't been dating in a long time,
so I'll tell you.

00:24:47.194 --> 00:24:49.029
You're, like, wrong about that.

00:24:49.321 --> 00:24:51.239
-Okay.
-Just flat-out wrong.

00:24:51.323 --> 00:24:52.157
Right.

00:24:53.116 --> 00:24:55.076
My phone's right here.
You can put it right in.

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:57.078
I'll give you my number. Yeah.

00:24:57.829 --> 00:25:01.249
-Yeah. And then you call me and I'll--
-Great. Oh, thank you.

00:25:01.333 --> 00:25:02.751
So, 917...

00:25:02.834 --> 00:25:05.879
This is without glasses.
This is, like, really dangerous.

00:25:05.962 --> 00:25:08.882
You're just stabbing at the numbers
and hoping it comes out right.

00:25:08.965 --> 00:25:11.426
So, can I give you a card?

00:25:12.928 --> 00:25:15.180
-Great. Thank you.
-Yeah, my pleasure.

00:25:15.263 --> 00:25:16.181
Thank you.

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:17.515
It was, um...

00:25:17.599 --> 00:25:19.142
It was... a lot of fun.

00:25:19.226 --> 00:25:20.519
Yeah, you're great.

00:25:21.478 --> 00:25:23.230
I think-- I do think you're lovely.

00:25:23.313 --> 00:25:25.982
I think you're--
I think you're crazy not to be dating.

00:25:26.066 --> 00:25:30.278
This sounds like you're not interested
in me, 'cause you're giving me advice

00:25:30.362 --> 00:25:32.489
-on how to find a date.
-No, well--

00:25:32.572 --> 00:25:34.783
-I'm reading between the lines.
-I mean, yeah, so...

00:25:34.866 --> 00:25:35.951
[sighs]

00:25:36.493 --> 00:25:37.577
[exhales]

00:25:40.163 --> 00:25:42.457
-And your name is Francine, right?
-Yes.

00:25:44.251 --> 00:25:45.543
[both laugh]

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:49.548
-But if an Eileen answers, don't hang up.
-Yeah.

00:25:49.631 --> 00:25:52.008
-Yeah, right. I got it.
-[laughs]

00:25:52.092 --> 00:25:55.303
Thank you so much for a lovely evening

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:57.889
and celebrating my birthday with me.

00:25:57.973 --> 00:25:59.224
Yeah. Yeah.

00:25:59.307 --> 00:26:01.309
-Um...
-So I'm not your type?

00:26:02.686 --> 00:26:03.812
I don't...

00:26:04.229 --> 00:26:05.063
[exhales]

00:26:05.146 --> 00:26:08.608
-I'm not insulted.
-No. I know you're not insulted.

00:26:08.692 --> 00:26:11.361
-It's just a feeling. I don't know.
-Right. Right.

00:26:11.444 --> 00:26:14.114
That's all I can say. It's just a feeling.

00:26:14.197 --> 00:26:17.242
It's got everything to do with me
and not you.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:26.501
[Gloria] I think it's better to know
than to have a complete mystery.

00:26:26.585 --> 00:26:28.253
-[Leonard] Oh, yeah. So do I.
-Okay.

00:26:28.336 --> 00:26:29.754
-Bye.
-[Leonard] Thanks. Bye.

00:26:31.256 --> 00:26:32.132
Take care.

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:37.804
Thank you.

00:26:43.727 --> 00:26:45.312
I hate this shit.

00:26:47.606 --> 00:26:49.524
-[Dianna] Very nice to have...
-[Leonard] Yeah.

00:26:49.608 --> 00:26:51.985
...spent the time with you.

00:26:52.068 --> 00:26:54.779
Yeah. And thanks for coming out
on your birthday.

00:26:57.324 --> 00:26:58.158
All right.

00:26:58.241 --> 00:26:59.451
-Goodnight.
-Goodnight.

00:27:00.285 --> 00:27:01.161
I got you.

00:27:02.412 --> 00:27:03.788
-Goodbye.
-Bye.

00:27:05.415 --> 00:27:07.208
-So, sweet dreams.
-Yeah. You too.

00:27:07.292 --> 00:27:08.376
-Okay.
-Okay.

00:27:08.668 --> 00:27:09.502
All right.

00:27:19.512 --> 00:27:21.181
[horns honking in the distance]

00:27:26.645 --> 00:27:28.688
["Elevate" by St. Lucia playing]

00:27:45.246 --> 00:27:47.374
♪ I don't know how you do it ♪

00:27:47.457 --> 00:27:51.086
♪ But somehow you always will be there ♪

00:27:55.173 --> 00:28:00.845
♪ You wait for the sun to make the sky ♪

00:28:01.513 --> 00:28:05.517
♪ And no one elevates you ♪

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:07.352
♪ Elevates you now ♪

00:28:07.435 --> 00:28:08.603
Hi!

00:28:09.521 --> 00:28:10.855
-How are you?
-Hi, good.

00:28:10.939 --> 00:28:13.149
-Do you want to go down to the park?
-Sure, I'd love to.

00:28:13.233 --> 00:28:14.067
Okay.

00:28:14.526 --> 00:28:17.612
-Glad you like it iced.
-Yes.

00:28:17.821 --> 00:28:19.656
'Cause otherwise...

00:28:19.989 --> 00:28:22.701
-my hands would be burning.
-Yes. Well, may I take it?

00:28:23.118 --> 00:28:25.036
-I guess you can.
-[laughs] Thank you.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:28.581
-Uh, skim milk?
-Yes, with a little bit of brown sugar.

00:28:29.249 --> 00:28:32.877
♪ And no one elevates you ♪

00:28:32.961 --> 00:28:34.671
♪ Elevates you now ♪

00:28:38.133 --> 00:28:42.137
♪ And no one's gonna take you ♪

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:44.222
♪ Gonna take you there ♪

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:51.146
♪ And no one elevates you ♪

00:28:51.271 --> 00:28:53.356
♪ Elevates you now ♪

00:28:59.779 --> 00:29:02.782
♪ Hold on to your heart ♪